<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gansu</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gansu is a province of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, stretching from the Loess Plateau along the narrow Hexi (Gansu) Corridor towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lanzhou">Lanzhou</a> as its capital. Its eastern valleys were the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a> of the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>, and the corridor carried the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> between China and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. Over the centuries it was contested by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetans">Tibetans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanguts">Tanguts</a>, and many short-lived states rose there during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixteen-kingdoms">Sixteen Kingdoms</a> period.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gansu.jpg" alt="Gansu" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Terry Wu · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hoodoos_at_Zhangye_Danxia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
